  "notes": "`high`, because the full signal chain and workflow are documented: MPC2000XL into Pro Tools eight tracks at a time, the drum bus through Studer A800 tape emulation, the master through an Ampex ATR-102, then a Neve 1073 to restore low-end bite, with an LA-2A used sparingly **to preserve knock**. \"Drums are the most important element in hip-hop, period\" is the stated philosophy and it is authored as a moderate-high `velocityTiers.main` and the three-lane `percs` set the tape bus is built to glue. **The genuinely distinguishing fact is that the material is increasingly original, written to sound sampled** — after learning that sampling cost him royalties — and then processed to imitate vinyl. A model cannot saturate, so what carries \"written to sound sampled\" is the shape: **2–4 bar loops on `i–iv` and `ii–V–i`** with a `question_answer` melody that states and answers within the loop, rather than a chopped figure that would need a source record to have existed. **Processing is chosen *during* composition, so timbre drives the harmony choices** — that is why `timbreHint` is authored at all here and why the chord voicing sits open and mid rather than close: horns, Rhodes and guitar in a register that leaves the tape bus room. The bass is **sample-style filtered with root/5th motion, saturated rather than heavily compressed**, which is exactly `boom_chick` at 0.74 — the third and least mechanical of the three `boom_chick` models in this batch, with `M2` and `m7` reaching past the root and fifth. **8-bar intro, scratch hooks, instrumental tags and DJ-oriented structures** are on `arrangement.sectionBars`.",
